      "definition": "A treatise from outside the tradition (Sanskrit bahiśāstra, for bahiḥśāstra; Tibetan phyi'i bstan bcos, 'outer treatise'), apparently a non-Buddhist work. Munidatta names one as the source of the verse he quotes on people who carefully do wrong and, giving up milk, drink poison (cp.46.m4). The entry for fools records his scorn for scholars proud of outer treatises.",
      "symbolicImage": "",
      "symbolicReadings": [],
      "forbiddenInLine": [],
      "rationale": "Munidatta's label for a quoted source outside the Buddhist teaching, which a reader of the woven commentary needs explained; 'outer treatise' follows the Tibetan and the wording already used in the glossary's definitions."
