  "questions": [
    "sabari-raga (new) vs sabari: the heading's rāga is রাগ শবরী (Tibetan sham+ba rI). The existing 'sabari' is the Śabara woman of song 28, with Nairātmā readings and forbidden words, so I proposed a separate rāga entry rather than adding the rāga's forms to her. 'sivari' (Śīvarī, song 26, Tibetan sha ba ri) may be the same melody; merge the two if you think so.",
    "shade, cp.46.4a: chāa māā kāa samāṇā likens the body to a shadow (chāyā; Tibetan grib ma) and an illusion; 'shade' reads oddly there. Suggest adding 'shadow' as a variant of 'shade'.",
    "visuddhi, cp.46.5a: ṣohia is a verb, 'purify' (Tibetan sbyang bar gyis; Munidatta viśodhana, cp.46.m6), but the entry's English is the noun 'purity'. Suggest adding 'purify' as a variant.",
    "coming-and-going: new reading for the refrain (2b), from cp.46.m3, which is unlabelled in the edition but comments on couplet 2: coming and going in saṃsāra (saṃsāre yātāyātaṃ), which breaks off if, even at the time consciousness passes over, one remembers the true guru's word and frees one's own mind of delusion; the Tibetan comment agrees ('khor ba'i 'gro ba dang 'ong ba rgyun 'chad par 'gyur). Add to the entry's readings?",
    "svapna / mirror / pratibimba: new reading from cp.46.m2 for couplet 1: as one's own appearance in a dream and a reflection in a mirror, so one is to see bhavavijñāna 'in between' (antarā). The Tibetan comment reads this as the consciousness of the intermediate existence (bar srid kyi rnam par shes pa) and has 'the person in a dream' (rmi lam gyi skyes bu) where the Sanskrit has svapratibhāsa. Worth recording under svapna and mirror?",
    "antarabhava, cp.46.1b: the Bengali has antarāle, 'in between' (candidate অন্তরালে); the Tibetan line has bar gyi srid pa, 'intermediate existence' (added as an AO form), and the Tibetan comment bar srid. I did not match অন্তরালে to antarabhava, since that would put the Tibetan's reading into the line; decide whether the song means the intermediate state.",
    "paramartha, cp.46.m4: Munidatta introduces couplet 3 as praise of the paramātmacitta, 'mind of the supreme self', and later calls it the mark of the paramārthacitta; the Tibetan has don dam pa'i sems, 'ultimate mind', both times. paramātma- may be a slip for paramārtha-; I proposed no form for either.",
    "Not proposed: in 3b (pekha moa mohe bali bali bājhai) the Tibetan line has sgyu ma la rmongs skra dkar gnyer 'jigs, apparently reading moa as māyā and bali as valī, 'wrinkle' (with white hair), where Munidatta has the dull-witted 'utterly bound in delusion' (cp.46.m4); moa and bali are left for the drafter to flag. Also left: phuḍaaṇa (5b; Tibetan gsal byed, Munidatta 'does not become otherwise').",
    "paksa, cp.46.m5: Munidatta's pakṣāpakṣabhinna, 'distinct from side and non-side', stands against the Tibetan's phyogs dang phyogs ma yin pa dbyer med pa, 'side and non-side inseparable', as if reading abhinna; a point for the draft and weave."
  ],
